{
  "result": "There is a bench at the train station that nobody seems to notice. It sits just past the ticket counter, under a flickering fluorescent light that has been flickering for at least three years. I notice it because I have spent a lot of time waiting. We all wait, but we rarely talk about what waiting does to a person.\n\nWaiting sharpens you. When you stand on a platform with nowhere to be for twenty minutes, you start to see things. The way a pigeon tilts its head before it steals a crumb. The way the woman across from you holds her coffee like an anchor. The schedule board clicks and hums, flipping letters with a soft, mechanical clack that feels oddly comforting, like the station itself is breathing.\n\nIn a world obsessed with speed, waiting feels like rebellion. We tap our feet, refresh our screens, sigh loudly to show the universe we are busy and important. But the bench doesn't care. It has held commuters, lovers saying goodbye, kids with backpacks bigger than themselves, and old men who come just to watch the trains arrive.\n\nMaybe that's why I like it there. The bench remembers what we try to forget, that life is mostly intervals, not events. The train always comes eventually. And while you wait, if you let yourself, you belong to no place at all, suspended between where you were and where you're going, which is a strangely free place to be.",
